/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048760 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050709 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048760 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050862 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048760 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186078577 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048760 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186078578 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048760 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186078579 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048760 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186078580 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048760 #edb/ref 1031 "Solving Linear Equations by Clearing a Rational Expression" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048760 #edb/ref 1033 651 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048760 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "bc360b9e-12dd-5337-833b-984ed3eed6ff" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049079 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048576 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049079 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048690 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049079 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049759 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049079 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186051061 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049079 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186068755 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049079 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186068756 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049079 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186068757 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049079 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186068758 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049079 #edb/ref 1031 "Multiplying Rational Expressions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049079 #edb/ref 1033 435 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049079 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "d8e5a8ad-8cb3-52d6-a4cb-aee78ee0ea22" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050211 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048323 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050211 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050050 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050211 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050474 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050211 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186083241 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050211 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186083242 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050211 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186083334 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050211 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186429575 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050211 #edb/ref 1031 "Advanced Rational Equations" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050211 #edb/ref 1033 708 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050211 #edb/ref 1034 0.7580943949689775 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050211 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "36b87c24-96f0-58b5-9017-03b96cf0c512" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068614 #edb/ref 1069 "e-5823" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068614 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve for $a$ where $\\dfrac{2a -5}{3a^2} + \\dfrac{1}{a} = \\dfrac{a-1}{2a^2}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068614 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8787186e-94bd-53da-9304-b0d3efbcb966" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068614 #edb/ref 1074 "First, note that $a = 0$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nNow, let's multiply both sides of the equation by the least common denominator. The equation has the denominators $3a^2,$ $a,$ and $2a^2,$ so the least common multiple (or $\\textrm{LCM}$) of the denominators is\n\n$$\n\\textrm{LCM}(3a^2, a, 2a^2) = 6a^2.\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the equation by $6a^2$ and simplifying, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2a-5}{3{a}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{a} & =\\frac{a-1}{2{a}^{2}} \\\\ 6{a}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{2a-5}{3{a}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{a}) & =6{a}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{a-1}{2{a}^{2}}) \\\\ 6{a}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{2a-5}{3{a}^{2}})+6{a}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{a}) & =6{a}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{a-1}{2{a}^{2}}) \\\\ 2\\cdot 3{a}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{2a-5}{3{a}^{2}})+6a\\cdot a\\cdot (\\frac{1}{a}) & =3\\cdot 2{a}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{a-1}{2{a}^{2}}) \\\\ 2\\cdot \\cancel{3{a}^{2}}\\cdot (\\frac{2a-5}{\\cancel{3{a}^{2}}})+6a\\cdot \\cancel{a}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{\\cancel{a}}) & =3\\cdot \\cancel{2{a}^{2}}\\cdot (\\frac{a-1}{\\cancel{2{a}^{2}}}) \\\\ 2\\cdot (2a-5)+6a\\cdot (1) & =3\\cdot (a-1) \\\\ 2(2a-5)+6a & =3(a-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $a,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(2a-5)+6a & =3(a-1) \\\\ 4a-10+6a & =3a-3 \\\\ 10a-10 & =3a-3 \\\\ 10a-3a & =-3+10 \\\\ 7a & =7 \\\\ a & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, recall that we cannot allow $a=0$ as a solution. Since $a=1$ is different from $a=0,$ we can accept it as a solution.\n\nTherefore, the solution is $a=1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068615 #edb/ref 1069 "e-5825" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068615 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve for $x$ where $3 - \\dfrac{10x+7}{8x}  = \\dfrac{6x-3}{2x}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068615 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "84d3492d-12bb-50c2-b5b4-5f08567eb4f5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068615 #edb/ref 1074 "First, note that $x=0$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined.\n\nNow, let's multiply both sides of the equation by the least common denominator. The equation has the denominators $8x$ and $2x,$ so the least common multiple (or $\\textrm{LCM}$) of the denominators is\n\n$$\n\\textrm{LCM}(8x,2x) = 8x.\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the equation by $8x$ and simplifying, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3-\\frac{10x+7}{8x} & =\\frac{6x-3}{2x} \\\\ 8x\\cdot (3-\\frac{10x+7}{8x}) & =8x\\cdot (\\frac{6x-3}{2x}) \\\\ 8x\\cdot 3-8x\\cdot (\\frac{10x+7}{8x}) & =8x\\cdot (\\frac{6x-3}{2x}) \\\\ 24x-\\cancel{8x}\\cdot (\\frac{10x+7}{\\cancel{8x}}) & =4\\cdot \\cancel{2x}\\cdot (\\frac{6x-3}{\\cancel{2x}}) \\\\ 24x-(10x+7) & =4\\cdot (6x-3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $x,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}24x-(10x+7) & =4\\cdot (6x-3) \\\\ 14x-7 & =24x-12 \\\\ 24x-14x & =-7+12 \\\\ 10x & =5 \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, recall that we cannot allow $x=0$ as a solution. Since $x= \\dfrac 12$ is different from $x=0,$ we accept it as a solution. \n\nTherefore, the solution is $x= \\dfrac 12.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068629 #edb/ref 1069 "e-6260" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068629 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve for $x$ where $\\dfrac{x+2}{x} = \\dfrac{x-4}{x} + 2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068629 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8e0fa2cd-2b29-510a-868f-4779de8536e5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068629 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we note that $x = 0$ is an excluded solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nNow, let's multiply both sides of the equation by the least common denominator. Both denominators in the equation are $x,$ so the least common denominator is $x.$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the equation by $x$ and simplifying, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+2}{x} & =\\frac{x-4}{x}+2 \\\\ x\\cdot (\\frac{x+2}{x}) & =x\\cdot (\\frac{x-4}{x}+2) \\\\ x\\cdot \\frac{x+2}{x} & =x\\cdot \\frac{x-4}{x}+x\\cdot 2 \\\\ \\cancel{x}\\cdot \\frac{x+2}{\\cancel{x}} & =\\cancel{x}\\cdot \\frac{x-4}{\\cancel{x}}+2x \\\\ x+2 & =x-4+2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $x,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2 & =x-4+2x \\\\ x+2 & =3x-4 \\\\ 3x-x & =2+4 \\\\ 2x & =6 \\\\ x & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, recall that we cannot allow $x=0$ as a solution. Since $x=3$ is different from $x=0,$ we accept it as a solution. \n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068630 #edb/ref 1069 "e-6261" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068630 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve for $x$ where $\\dfrac{1+x}{x} + \\dfrac{5+x}{6x} = \\dfrac{1-x}{3x}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068630 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5d20e960-7554-5a05-b1e0-2b98b944e186" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068630 #edb/ref 1074 "First, note that $x = 0$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nNow, let's multiply both sides of the equation by the least common denominator. The equation has the denominators $x, 6x,$ and $3x,$ so the the least common multiple (or $\\textrm{LCM}$) of the denominators is\n\n$$\n\\textrm{LCM}(x, 6x, 3x) = 6x.\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the equation by $6x$ and simplifying, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1+x}{x}+\\frac{5+x}{6x} & =\\frac{1-x}{3x} \\\\ 6x\\cdot (\\frac{1+x}{x}+\\frac{5+x}{6x}) & =6x\\cdot \\frac{1-x}{3x} \\\\ 6x\\cdot (\\frac{1+x}{x})+6x\\cdot (\\frac{5+x}{6x}) & =6x\\cdot (\\frac{1-x}{3x}) \\\\ 6x\\cdot (\\frac{1+x}{x})+6x\\cdot (\\frac{5+x}{6x}) & =2\\cdot 3x\\cdot (\\frac{1-x}{3x}) \\\\ 6\\cancel{x}\\cdot (\\frac{1+x}{\\cancel{x}})+\\cancel{6x}\\cdot (\\frac{5+x}{\\cancel{6x}}) & =2\\cdot \\cancel{3x}\\cdot (\\frac{1-x}{\\cancel{3x}}) \\\\ 6(1+x)+(5+x) & =2(1-x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $x,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6(1+x)+(5+x) & =2(1-x) \\\\ 6+6x+5+x & =2-2x \\\\ 7x+11 & =2-2x \\\\ 7x+2x & =2-11 \\\\ 9x & =-9 \\\\ x & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, recall that we cannot allow $x=0$ as a solution. Since $x=-1$ is different from $x=0,$ we can accept it as the solution.\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=-1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068880 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048760 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068880 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186069066 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068880 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186069516 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068880 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186069519 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068880 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186250481 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068880 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186250482 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068880 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186250483 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068880 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186345134 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068880 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186345135 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068880 #edb/ref 1039 "Solving Rational Equations Containing One Distinct Linear Denominator" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068880 #edb/r